1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,516
<font color="#000000">How do you pick the right attention mechanism for a
long-range Transformer task,</font>

2
00:00:05,596 --> 00:00:08,574
<font color="#000000">and could mixing several types work even better?</font>

3
00:00:08,654 --> 00:00:10,039
<font color="#000000">This paper, DARTFormer,</font>

4
00:00:10,119 --> 00:00:14,753
<font color="#000000">adapts differentiable neural architecture search to
answer both questions.</font>

5
00:00:14,833 --> 00:00:20,486
<font color="#000000">The authors build a supernetwork that contains many
efficient attention types in parallel,</font>

6
00:00:20,566 --> 00:00:24,499
<font color="#000000">then use a masked validation accuracy drop metric
to rank them.</font>

7
00:00:24,579 --> 00:00:28,640
<font color="#000000">Their search reliably finds the best single
attention for a task,</font>

8
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:34,182
<font color="#000000">but a surprising second result emerges:
heterogeneous Transformers that combine several</font>

9
00:00:34,262 --> 00:00:37,430
<font color="#000000">attention types beat the average homogeneous model,</font>

10
00:00:37,510 --> 00:00:39,660
<font color="#000000">yet never beat the single best one.</font>

11
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:45,397
<font color="#000000">The Transformer's original dot-product attention
scales quadratically with sequence length,</font>

12
00:00:45,477 --> 00:00:48,803
<font color="#000000">so a large family of efficient alternatives has appeared,</font>

13
00:00:48,883 --> 00:00:52,866
<font color="#000000">from Longformer and Bigbird to Performer, Reformer,
and Synthesizer.</font>

14
00:00:52,946 --> 00:00:58,064
<font color="#000000">The catch is that no single one wins everywhere:
earlier long-range benchmarking showed</font>

15
00:00:58,144 --> 00:01:02,904
<font color="#000000">that the best attention depends heavily on the task
when there is no pretraining.</font>

16
00:01:02,984 --> 00:01:05,951
<font color="#000000">That leaves practitioners with an awkward question,</font>

17
00:01:06,031 --> 00:01:11,149
<font color="#000000">namely how to efficiently discover which attention
is right for a given long-range task</font>

18
00:01:11,229 --> 00:01:13,300
<font color="#000000">without simply training all of them.</font>

19
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:16,966
<font color="#000000">There is a long-standing intuition that each
attention head can specialize,</font>

20
00:01:17,046 --> 00:01:20,331
<font color="#000000">learning a different kind of relationship,</font>

21
00:01:20,411 --> 00:01:23,697
<font color="#000000">much as convolutional kernels learn different features.</font>

22
00:01:23,777 --> 00:01:27,062
<font color="#000000">If that is true,</font>

23
00:01:27,142 --> 00:01:30,428
<font color="#000000">then a Transformer mixing several attention types
could learn a richer set of relationships</font>

24
00:01:30,508 --> 00:01:33,793
<font color="#000000">and outperform any single-attention model.</font>

25
00:01:33,873 --> 00:01:37,159
<font color="#000000">This paper takes that intuition seriously and asks
directly whether the optimal attention</font>

26
00:01:37,239 --> 00:01:40,524
<font color="#000000">for a task is actually a mixture of different attentions,</font>

27
00:01:40,604 --> 00:01:43,890
<font color="#000000">rather than assuming the answer.</font>

28
00:01:44,270 --> 00:01:48,100
<font color="#000000">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

29
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:52,010
<font color="#000000">First, it proposes DARTFormer,</font>

30
00:01:52,090 --> 00:01:55,920
<font color="#000000">a differentiable-architecture-search-style method
that efficiently finds the best attention for a task.</font>

31
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,830
<font color="#000000">Second,</font>

32
00:01:59,910 --> 00:02:03,740
<font color="#000000">it extends that framework to build and search for
heterogeneous Transformers that combine multiple attention types,</font>

33
00:02:03,820 --> 00:02:07,650
<font color="#000000">using two procedures: an expensive iterative
pruning method they call NAS Prune,</font>

34
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:11,560
<font color="#000000">and a cheap single-pass method called NAS One-shot.</font>

35
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:15,470
<font color="#000000">Third, and most importantly,</font>

36
00:02:15,550 --> 00:02:19,380
<font color="#000000">it shows empirically that these heterogeneous
Transformers cannot outperform the best</font>

37
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:23,290
<font color="#000000">homogeneous Transformer on the long-range NLP tasks
studied.</font>

38
00:02:23,670 --> 00:02:29,012
<font color="#000000">DARTFormer builds a single-layer supernetwork that
holds one candidate block for every</font>

39
00:02:29,092 --> 00:02:31,344
<font color="#000000">efficient attention type in parallel.</font>

40
00:02:31,424 --> 00:02:35,883
<font color="#000000">Borrowing the fixed-alpha trick from differentiable
architecture search,</font>

41
00:02:35,963 --> 00:02:40,360
<font color="#000000">it does not learn softmax edge weights and skips
bi-level optimization;</font>

42
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:43,827
<font color="#000000">instead it simply averages the candidate block outputs,</font>

43
00:02:43,907 --> 00:02:47,673
<font color="#000000">which lets the supernetwork train all the way to
convergence.</font>

44
00:02:47,753 --> 00:02:49,123
<font color="#000000">To rank the attentions,</font>

45
00:02:49,203 --> 00:02:53,473
<font color="#000000">it uses a masked validation accuracy drop metric:
mask out one block,</font>

46
00:02:53,553 --> 00:02:56,120
<font color="#000000">measure how far validation accuracy falls,</font>

47
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:00,029
<font color="#000000">and the block whose removal hurts the most is
judged the best.</font>

48
00:03:00,109 --> 00:03:05,010
<font color="#000000">For homogeneous models the top-scoring block is
chosen; for heterogeneous ones,</font>

49
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:08,414
<font color="#000000">blocks are either pruned worst-first with fine-tuning,</font>

50
00:03:08,494 --> 00:03:12,890
<font color="#000000">or the top four are taken directly and stacked into
a full Transformer.</font>

51
00:03:13,270 --> 00:03:18,282
<font color="#000000">All experiments run on three tasks drawn from the
Long Range Arena benchmark,</font>

52
00:03:18,362 --> 00:03:20,927
<font color="#000000">chosen to stress long-sequence modeling.</font>

53
00:03:21,007 --> 00:03:27,144
<font color="#000000">These are byte-level binary text classification on
IMDb with sequences of one thousand tokens,</font>

54
00:03:27,224 --> 00:03:31,442
<font color="#000000">ListOps ten-way classification with
two-thousand-token sequences,</font>

55
00:03:31,522 --> 00:03:35,939
<font color="#000000">and byte-level document matching with
four-thousand-token sequences.</font>

56
00:03:36,019 --> 00:03:40,965
<font color="#000000">The search considers nine candidate attention
mechanisms, including Bigbird,</font>

57
00:03:41,045 --> 00:03:46,256
<font color="#000000">Linear Transformer, Linformer, Local attention,
Longformer, Performer, Reformer,</font>

58
00:03:46,336 --> 00:03:48,636
<font color="#000000">Sparse Transformer, and Synthesizer,</font>

59
00:03:48,716 --> 00:03:53,530
<font color="#000000">with hyperparameters largely matching the original
Long Range Arena setup.</font>

60
00:03:53,910 --> 00:03:55,990
<font color="#000000">The headline finding splits in two.</font>

61
00:03:56,070 --> 00:03:57,286
<font color="#000000">On the positive side,</font>

62
00:03:57,366 --> 00:04:02,533
<font color="#000000">the masked-validation-drop search is genuinely
effective at homogeneous selection: it</font>

63
00:04:02,613 --> 00:04:07,470
<font color="#000000">correctly identifies the best attention for text
classification and for ListOps,</font>

64
00:04:07,550 --> 00:04:14,568
<font color="#000000">where Reformer stands out with a drop score of
nearly twelve while every other attention scores below half a point.</font>

65
00:04:14,648 --> 00:04:15,864
<font color="#000000">On the negative side,</font>

66
00:04:15,944 --> 00:04:21,172
<font color="#000000">when the authors assemble heterogeneous
Transformers that mix several attention types,</font>

67
00:04:21,252 --> 00:04:25,924
<font color="#000000">none of them beats the best single-attention model
on any of the three tasks.</font>

68
00:04:26,004 --> 00:04:30,430
<font color="#000000">Mixing attention helps you beat the average choice,
but not the best one.</font>

69
00:04:30,810 --> 00:04:36,152
<font color="#000000">A useful practical takeaway comes from comparing
the two heterogeneous search procedures.</font>

70
00:04:36,232 --> 00:04:38,040
<font color="#000000">The expensive NAS Prune method,</font>

71
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:41,451
<font color="#000000">which repeatedly removes the worst block and fine-tunes,</font>

72
00:04:41,531 --> 00:04:46,751
<font color="#000000">offers no consistent advantage over the far cheaper
NAS One-shot method that just takes</font>

73
00:04:46,831 --> 00:04:49,431
<font color="#000000">the top-scoring attentions in a single pass,</font>

74
00:04:49,511 --> 00:04:52,782
<font color="#000000">at least when good attentions are correctly identified.</font>

75
00:04:52,862 --> 00:04:58,203
<font color="#000000">Attempts to tilt the mixture toward the strongest
attentions or give them more heads also</font>

76
00:04:58,283 --> 00:05:00,335
<font color="#000000">failed to produce consistent gains,</font>

77
00:05:00,415 --> 00:05:03,320
<font color="#000000">reinforcing that the cheap method is good enough.</font>

78
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:06,356
<font color="#000000">To put concrete numbers on it: on ListOps,</font>

79
00:05:06,436 --> 00:05:11,110
<font color="#000000">Reformer earns a masked-validation-drop score of
eleven point eight five,</font>

80
00:05:11,190 --> 00:05:15,083
<font color="#000000">dwarfing every other attention's score of under
half a point,</font>

81
00:05:15,163 --> 00:05:17,428
<font color="#000000">a strikingly clean selection signal.</font>

82
00:05:17,508 --> 00:05:21,466
<font color="#000000">On document matching, the best homogeneous model,
Synthesizer,</font>

83
00:05:21,546 --> 00:05:23,941
<font color="#000000">reaches seventy-one point one percent,</font>

84
00:05:24,021 --> 00:05:31,366
<font color="#000000">while the heterogeneous NAS Prune and NAS One-shot
models trail at sixty-seven and sixty-four point seven percent.</font>

85
00:05:31,446 --> 00:05:37,163
<font color="#000000">On text classification the best homogeneous
Performer hits sixty-four point five percent,</font>

86
00:05:37,243 --> 00:05:40,550
<font color="#000000">and again neither heterogeneous variant edges ahead.</font>

87
00:05:40,630 --> 00:05:45,500
<font color="#000000">Across nine attention mechanisms and three tasks,
the pattern is consistent.</font>

88
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:49,920
<font color="#000000">For a given task it is often unclear which
attention will do best,</font>

89
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,792
<font color="#000000">and DARTFormer offers a cheap,</font>

90
00:05:51,872 --> 00:05:57,472
<font color="#000000">reliable recipe: train a single-layer
mixed-attention Transformer and use masked validation</font>

91
00:05:57,552 --> 00:05:59,532
<font color="#000000">accuracy drop to pick the winner,</font>

92
00:05:59,612 --> 00:06:05,150
<font color="#000000">keeping in mind that very low drop scores are a
warning that the choice may be unreliable.</font>

93
00:06:05,230 --> 00:06:08,396
<font color="#000000">The deeper lesson is a cautionary one for the field.</font>

94
00:06:08,476 --> 00:06:13,077
<font color="#000000">Combining diverse attention mechanisms does beat
the average single choice,</font>

95
00:06:13,157 --> 00:06:15,699
<font color="#000000">but it never beats the best single choice,</font>

96
00:06:15,779 --> 00:06:22,690
<font color="#000000">which suggests the different attentions do not
simply add complementary biases the way common intuition assumes.</font>
